    "result_text": "The full suite is green: **BUILD SUCCESS**, 3 tests run, 0 failures/errors.\n\n## Summary\n\nI introduced address normalization applied whenever an owner is created (`POST /api/owners`).\n\n**New file \u2014 `OwnerAddress.java`** (in the owner function package, mirroring the existing `OwnerEmail`/`OwnerTelephone` helper pattern): a package-private `normalize(String)` that trims, collapses internal whitespace to single spaces, upper-cases, and expands whole-token street abbreviations (`ST\u2192STREET`, `RD\u2192ROAD`, `AVE\u2192AVENUE`). Abbreviation expansion is token-based (splitting on spaces), so it can't corrupt substrings like the `ST` inside `STREET`. A null/blank input normalizes to `\"\"`.\n\n**`RequireOwnerFields.java`** \u2014 the first pipeline step now normalizes the address (`request.setAddress(OwnerAddress.normalize(...))`) *before* the required-field check runs, and republishes the mutated body via the existing `Out<OwnerFieldsDto>`. This single change satisfies every requirement of the spec:\n\n- **Blank-after-normalization is rejected** \u2014 a whitespace-only address normalizes to `\"\"`, which the existing `isBlank` check catches, producing the 400.\n- **Stored and returned as normalized** \u2014 `BuildOwner`/`SaveOwner` and the response mapper read `getAddress()` from the republished/persisted entity, so the normalized form is what's stored and returned.\n- **Comparisons use the normalized form** \u2014 `RequireUniqueHousehold` reads the republished `@Val` body (normalized address), and the mapper's `Household.id(...)` reads the stored normalized address; both are computed over normalized values consistently.\n\nTest results (`AcceptanceTest`): whitespace/case (`\"  12  main  st \"` \u2192 `\"12 MAIN STREET\"`), abbreviation expansion (`\"7 elm ave\"` \u2192 `\"7 ELM AVENUE\"`), and blank rejection (`\"   \"` \u2192 400) all pass, and no existing tests regressed.",
